export const pkUnit06CommonInfoMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PK Unit 6: Toys and Space — Common Info\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview\n\nPK Unit 6: Toys and Space helps children use toys, containers, classroom materials, and simple spatial routines to develop object handling, symbolic play, construction, turn-taking, problem solving, and early spatial awareness. Compared with PG, PK children can participate in more intentional toy exchange, longer pretend play, more stable clean-up routines, and simple spatial responses such as `in`, `out`, `on`, and `under`.\n\nThis unit should still remain concrete and play-based. Children are not expected to explain spatial relationships, answer all `Where is...?` questions verbally, or produce full spatial sentences. The focus is real toy play, object placement, in/out/on/under actions, open/close routines, give/take/wait, building, and toy clean-up.\n\nThe core PK language is grounded in action: `toy ｜ ball ｜ car ｜ block ｜ teddy ｜ doll ｜ train ｜ box ｜ bag ｜ basket ｜ in ｜ out ｜ on ｜ under ｜ open ｜ close ｜ give ｜ take ｜ wait ｜ clean up`. Children may use single words or short chunks such as `in the box`, `on the table`, `under the chair`, `my turn`, or `thank you` when ready.\n\n## 2. Unit Language\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary\n\n```text\ntoy ｜ toys ｜ ball ｜ car ｜ block ｜ teddy ｜ doll ｜ train ｜ box ｜ bag ｜ basket ｜ table ｜ chair ｜ shelf ｜ in ｜ out ｜ on ｜ under ｜ open ｜ close ｜ give ｜ take ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ my turn ｜ clean up ｜ build ｜ road ｜ house\n```\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks\n\n```text\nShow me the ball. ｜ Give me the car. ｜ Take the teddy. ｜ Open the box. ｜ Close the box. ｜ Put it in the box. ｜ Take it out. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Put it under the chair. ｜ Where is the toy? ｜ In or out? ｜ On or under? ｜ Your turn. ｜ My turn. ｜ Wait. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Toys in the basket. ｜ Build a road. ｜ Build a house. ｜ Thank you.\n```\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes\n\n### Week 1: Toys and Toy Choices\n\nChildren revisit familiar toys and expand toy recognition through ball, car, block, teddy, doll, train, and toy baskets. The focus is showing, choosing, naming attempts, and safe handling.\n\n### Week 2: In, Out, Open and Close\n\nChildren explore containers through boxes, bags, baskets, and toy homes. The focus is open/close, put in, take out, in/out choice, and object return.\n\n### Week 3: On, Under and Toy Places\n\nChildren extend spatial play to on/under routines with tables, chairs, shelves, mats, roads, and toy houses. The focus is action response and concrete placement, not verbal explanation.\n\n### Week 4: Build, Share and Clean Up Toys\n\nChildren integrate toy naming, spatial routines, turn-taking, construction, pretend play, and clean-up through toy roads, houses, garages, shops, and shared play spaces.\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool\n\n### Toy Input\n\ntoy ｜ toys ｜ ball ｜ car ｜ block ｜ teddy ｜ doll ｜ train ｜ box ｜ bag ｜ basket ｜ road ｜ house ｜ garage ｜ show me the toy ｜ choose a toy\n\n### Spatial and Container Input\n\nin ｜ out ｜ on ｜ under ｜ open ｜ close ｜ table ｜ chair ｜ shelf ｜ put it in ｜ take it out ｜ put it on the table ｜ put it under the chair ｜ in the basket ｜ on the shelf\n\n### Social and Routine Input\n\ngive ｜ take ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ my turn ｜ thank you ｜ clean up ｜ toys in ｜ put it here ｜ build ｜ share ｜ all done ｜ good job\n\n### Teacher Routine Language\n\nLook. ｜ Show me the ball. ｜ Choose a toy. ｜ Open the box. ｜ Close the box. ｜ Put it in. ｜ Take it out. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Put it under the chair. ｜ Where is the toy? ｜ Your turn. ｜ Wait. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Toys in the basket. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes\n\nChildren may respond by looking, touching, choosing, naming or attempting toy words, showing toys, giving and taking toys, opening and closing containers, placing toys in/out/on/under, answering with pointing or short chunks, building with blocks, pushing cars on roads, putting dolls or teddies in houses, waiting briefly, saying `my turn` or `your turn` when ready, and joining toy clean-up routines.\n\nChildren are not expected to explain spatial relationships, consistently answer `Where is...?` verbally, produce full sentences such as `The car is under the table`, follow multi-step spatial instructions without support, or share independently without teacher scaffolding.\n\n## 6. Theme Songs / Chants\n\n### Core Songs\n\nMystery Box #1  \nMystery Box #2  \nMystery Box #3  \nWhat's This What's That  \nWhere Is Baby  \nPeekaboo, I Love You  \nThe Shape Song #1  \nThe Shape Song #2  \nWind The Bobbin Up  \nDriving In My Car  \nThe Wheels on the Bus  \nClean Up Song\n\n### Use of Songs\n\nSongs should support mystery-object reveal, open/close, peekaboo, in/out, toy identification, vehicle movement, block play, clean-up, and turn-taking routines. Children may sing repeated toy or action words when ready, but object handling and movement remain valid participation.\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces\n\nClassroom  \nLego area  \nConstruction / block room  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nIndoor physical fitness area  \nSandpit when appropriate  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nTable activity area  \nToy shelf and classroom storage  \nTheatre / pretend play space\n\n### Useful Materials\n\nBalls  \nToy cars  \nBlocks  \nLego / Duplo bricks  \nLarge wooden blocks  \nLarge soft blocks  \nTeddies  \nDolls  \nTrains  \nToy roads / tracks  \nBoxes  \nBags  \nBaskets  \nTransparent storage boxes  \nShelves  \nTables  \nChairs  \nRoad mats  \nToy houses  \nGarages  \nScarves / covering fabric  \nMystery bags  \nVisual clean-up cards  \nTurn-taking tokens\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities\n\nThe Lego area and construction room can support roads, garages, houses, shelves, bridges, and toy homes. Boxes and baskets remain essential for in/out and clean-up routines. Tables, chairs, shelves, and mats provide concrete on/under spatial contexts. Outdoor spaces can support larger car-road or ball play if safety boundaries are clear.\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes\n\nThis unit should not become a formal grammar lesson on prepositions. `in`, `out`, `on`, and `under` should be experienced through actions and play.\n\nThis unit should not require full sentence output. Short chunks such as `in the box` or `on the table` are readiness-based.\n\nThis unit should not treat sharing as independent moral performance. Turn-taking requires adult scaffolding and clear visual cues.\n\nThis unit should not rely on worksheets or picture-only spatial tasks. Space must be experienced physically through toys, containers, furniture, and movement.\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus\n\nTeachers observe whether children recognise and choose familiar toys, use or attempt toy words, follow in/out/on/under actions with support, open and close containers, build toy spaces, participate in give/take/wait routines, use short social chunks when ready, and join clean-up with increasing independence.\n\nObservation should focus on object handling, spatial action response, symbolic play, turn-taking, construction, clean-up responsibility, and readiness-based short output rather than full spatial language mastery.\n",
  "zh": "# PK Unit 6: Toys and Space — Common Info｜PK 单元 6：玩具与空间 — 通用信息\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview｜单元主题概览\n\nPK 单元 6：玩具与空间帮助儿童使用玩具、容器、课堂材料和简单的空间常规来发展物品处理、象征性游戏、建构、轮流、问题解决和早期空间意识。和 PG 相比，PK 儿童可以参与更有意识的玩具交换、更长的假装游戏、更稳定的收拾常规，以及简单的空间回应，例如 `in`、`out`、`on`、`under`。\n\n本单元仍应保持具体和基于游戏。不要求儿童解释空间关系、口头回答所有\"Where is...?\"问题或产出完整空间句。重点是真实玩具游戏、物品放置、in／out／on／under 动作、打开／合上常规、给予／拿取／等待、搭建和玩具收拾。\n\n核心 PK 语言扎根于动作：`toy ｜ ball ｜ car ｜ block ｜ teddy ｜ doll ｜ train ｜ box ｜ bag ｜ basket ｜ in ｜ out ｜ on ｜ under ｜ open ｜ close ｜ give ｜ take ｜ wait ｜ clean up`。儿童在准备好时可以使用单个词或简短句块，例如 `in the box`、`on the table`、`under the chair`、`my turn`、`thank you`。\n\n## 2. Unit Language｜单元语言\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary｜PK 核心词汇\n\ntoy ｜ toys ｜ ball ｜ car ｜ block ｜ teddy ｜ doll ｜ train ｜ box ｜ bag ｜ basket ｜ table ｜ chair ｜ shelf ｜ in ｜ out ｜ on ｜ under ｜ open ｜ close ｜ give ｜ take ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ my turn ｜ clean up ｜ build ｜ road ｜ house\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks｜实用教师句块\n\nShow me the ball. ｜ Give me the car. ｜ Take the teddy. ｜ Open the box. ｜ Close the box. ｜ Put it in the box. ｜ Take it out. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Put it under the chair. ｜ Where is the toy? ｜ In or out? ｜ On or under? ｜ Your turn. ｜ My turn. ｜ Wait. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Toys in the basket. ｜ Build a road. ｜ Build a house. ｜ Thank you.\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes｜周次子主题\n\n### Week 1: Toys and Toy Choices｜第一周：玩具和玩具选择\n\n儿童重新接触熟悉的玩具并通过球、车、积木、泰迪、娃娃、火车和玩具篮扩展玩具识别。重点是展示、选择、命名尝试和安全处理。\n\n### Week 2: In, Out, Open and Close｜第二周：放入、取出、打开和合上\n\n儿童通过盒子、包、篮子和玩具家探索容器。重点是打开／合上、放入、取出、in／out 选择以及物品归位。\n\n### Week 3: On, Under and Toy Places｜第三周：在…上、在…下和玩具的位置\n\n儿童把空间游戏扩展到包含桌子、椅子、架子、垫子、道路和玩具家的 on／under 常规。重点是动作反应和具体放置，而非口头解释。\n\n### Week 4: Build, Share and Clean Up Toys｜第四周：搭建、分享和收拾玩具\n\n儿童通过玩具道路、房子、车库、商店和共享游戏空间把玩具命名、空间常规、轮流、建构、假装游戏和收拾整合起来。\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool｜教师输入库\n\n### Toy Input｜玩具输入\n\ntoy ｜ toys ｜ ball ｜ car ｜ block ｜ teddy ｜ doll ｜ train ｜ box ｜ bag ｜ basket ｜ road ｜ house ｜ garage ｜ show me the toy ｜ choose a toy\n\n### Spatial and Container Input｜空间与容器输入\n\nin ｜ out ｜ on ｜ under ｜ open ｜ close ｜ table ｜ chair ｜ shelf ｜ put it in ｜ take it out ｜ put it on the table ｜ put it under the chair ｜ in the basket ｜ on the shelf\n\n### Social and Routine Input｜社交与常规输入\n\ngive ｜ take ｜ wait ｜ your turn ｜ my turn ｜ thank you ｜ clean up ｜ toys in ｜ put it here ｜ build ｜ share ｜ all done ｜ good job\n\n### Teacher Routine Language｜教师常规语言\n\nLook. ｜ Show me the ball. ｜ Choose a toy. ｜ Open the box. ｜ Close the box. ｜ Put it in. ｜ Take it out. ｜ Put it on the table. ｜ Put it under the chair. ｜ Where is the toy? ｜ Your turn. ｜ Wait. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Toys in the basket. ｜ Thank you. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes｜预期 PK 反应方式\n\n儿童可能通过以下方式反应：看、触摸、选择、命名或尝试玩具词、展示玩具、给予和拿取玩具、打开和合上容器、把玩具放在 in／out／on／under、用指或简短句块回答、用积木搭建、把车推到道路上、把娃娃或泰迪放进房子里、短暂等待、在准备好时说 `my turn` 或 `your turn`，以及加入玩具收拾常规。\n\n不要求儿童解释空间关系、一致地口头回答\"Where is...?\"、产出完整句子例如 `The car is under the table`、在没有支持下跟随多步空间指令，或在没有教师支持的情况下独立分享。\n\n## 6. Core Unit Songs｜单元核心歌曲\n\n### Core Songs｜核心歌曲\n\nMystery Box #1  \nMystery Box #2  \nMystery Box #3  \nWhat's This What's That  \nWhere Is Baby  \nPeekaboo, I Love You  \nThe Shape Song #1  \nThe Shape Song #2  \nWind The Bobbin Up  \nDriving In My Car  \nThe Wheels on the Bus  \nClean Up Song\n\n### Use of Songs｜歌曲的使用\n\n歌曲应当支持神秘物品揭示、打开／合上、躲猫猫、放入／取出、玩具识别、车辆移动、积木游戏、收拾和轮流。儿童在准备好时可以唱重复的玩具或动作词，但物品处理和动作仍是有效的参与。\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities｜资源机会\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces｜可用 EASTIE 空间\n\nClassroom  \nLego area  \nConstruction / block room  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nIndoor physical fitness area  \nSandpit when appropriate  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nTable activity area  \nToy shelf and classroom storage  \nTheatre / pretend play space\n\n### Useful Materials｜可用材料\n\nBalls  \nToy cars  \nBlocks  \nLego / Duplo bricks  \nLarge wooden blocks  \nLarge soft blocks  \nTeddies  \nDolls  \nTrains  \nToy roads / tracks  \nBoxes  \nBags  \nBaskets  \nTransparent storage boxes  \nShelves  \nTables  \nChairs  \nRoad mats  \nToy houses  \nGarages  \nScarves / covering fabric  \nMystery bags  \nVisual clean-up cards  \nTurn-taking tokens\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities｜拓展资源机会\n\nLego 区和建构室可以支持道路、车库、房子、架子、桥梁和玩具家。盒子和篮子对于 in／out 和收拾常规仍然必不可少。桌子、椅子、架子和垫子提供具体的 on／under 空间背景。户外空间可以在安全边界清晰的情况下支持更大的车道路或球类游戏。\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes｜边界说明\n\n本单元不应变成介词的正式语法课。\"in\"、\"out\"、\"on\" 和 \"under\" 应通过动作和游戏来体验。\n\n本单元不应要求完整句子输出。简短句块例如 `in the box` 或 `on the table` 是准备度导向的。\n\n本单元不应把分享当作独立的道德表现。轮流需要成人支持和清晰的视觉提示。\n\n本单元不应依赖练习册或仅图片的空间任务。空间必须通过玩具、容器、家具和动作来身体体验。\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus｜单元观察重点\n\n教师观察儿童是否识别并选择熟悉的玩具、使用或尝试玩具词、在支持下跟随 in／out／on／under 动作、打开和合上容器、搭建玩具空间、参与给予／拿取／等待常规、在准备好时使用简短社交句块，以及越来越独立地加入收拾。\n\n观察应聚焦于物品处理、空间动作反应、象征性游戏、轮流、建构、收拾责任感和准备度导向的简短输出，而非完整空间语言掌握。"
};

